title: Jevons Paradox
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki
url: 
wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jevons_paradox
worldcat: 
year: 

When something gets more efficient to use, we often end up using more of it, not less. Cheaper, more efficient use of a resource (through new technology or policy) makes it more attractive, so demand rises, and total consumption can climb past what the efficiency gains saved.

Often called the best-known paradox in environmental economics. Governments and [[environmentalists|Environmentalism]] tend to assume efficiency lowers consumption, and overlook this rebound.

Worth keeping in mind whenever "more efficient" gets offered as the answer to a sustainability problem.

